  "details": "<p>This\u00a0<span class=\"highlightedSearchTerm\">archive</span>\u00a0is located in the\u00a0Northwest Palace.</p>\r\n<p>\u00a0<img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"362\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan66_general_plan_Kalhu1-17_p144.png\" width=\"392\" /></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"528\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan69_northwest_palace_Kalhu6-11_p149.png\" width=\"401\" /></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Most documents found in the Northwest Palace, built by the Assyrian king Ashurnarsipal II (883\u2013859 BC), predate the move of the new Assyrian capital to Dur-\u0160arrukin under Sargon II (721\u2013705 BC). The archive found in Rooms ZT 13\u201317 was written between 687 BC and 614 BC, thus postdating this move. This repository contained about sixty clay tablets, specifically loan documents, the sale of slaves and land, juridical texts, marriage contracts, and administrative lists.",
